  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 505 AM EDT MON AUG 10 2026\n\nThe most current model data continues to present a pattern aloft \nthat favors the formation of storm clusters - any of which could \nintrude on the weather for eastern Kentucky through the bulk of\nthe week. Right now, SPC has Tuesday highlighted as the greatest \nconcern for severe weather (damaging winds) from this development \nwith a Slight Risk outlined in our northeast counties - will\ncontinue to highlight this in messaging and social media. Please \nkeep monitoring this developing, potentially impactful, weather \nsituation.\n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nEastern Kentucky is still poised to experience rather active weather \nduring the long term forecast period, although specific details \nremain shrouded in mesoscale uncertainty. Confidence is increasing \nthat swaths of very heavy rainfall will set up across portions of \nthe Ohio River Valley as a significant pattern change takes place. \nAn amplifying ridge over the South-Central CONUS will budge up \nagainst the forecast area on Tuesday and turn the flow aloft \nnorthwesterly. A series of low-predictability shortwave disturbances \nwill ride around the periphery of that ridge and produce multiple \norganized thunderstorm complexes known as MCSes (Mesoscale \nConvective Systems). Seasonably warm temperatures (highs in the \nmid/upper 80s and lows near 70) and a very moist airmass (dewpoints \nin the 70s and PWATs between 1.7 and 1.9 inches) combine to provide \nmore than enough instability for strong thunderstorms. Each passing \nshortwave will provide sufficient shear for these organized storm \ncomplexes, which could modify their own environment as they evolve. \nTextbook theory suggests that the SW side of each complex could be a \nprime area for renewed development with subsequent rounds, and we \nwill also need to monitor for any residual outflow boundaries left \nbehind by earlier rounds. While the first rounds of activity are \nprogged to primarily affect NE KY and the Big Sandy Basin, the \nthreat should shift southwest over time. This is reflected in the \nSPC and WPC outlooks. \n\nWith the afternoon forecast update, SPC added a Slight Risk of \nsevere weather on Tuesday for most of the JKL CWA. Damaging wind \ngusts are the favored convective hazard type given the MCS/clustered \nstorm mode, but mesoscale boundaries could yield a localized risk \nfor additional hazard types. Any severe weather risk will be \naccompanied by a threat for excessive rainfall, and WPC has \nmaintained a Slight (Level 2/4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook for NE KY \non Tuesday. These NE locales will be the areas with the potential \nfor antecedently wet grounds from activity during the short term \nforecast period. Given the more widespread coverage of strong storms \nand heavy rain on Tuesday though, the Slight ERO expands to the \nentire CWA on Wednesday. Flash flooding is most likely wherever \nstorms engage in training and/or backbuilding behaviors. So, we will \nneed to monitor the output of higher-resolution forecast models for \nparameter spacing that favors said behaviors.\n\nAll of this activity will culminate in the approach of a cold front \nin the latter stages of the period. In general, increased late week \ncloud cover should work to suppress the early week heat as time \npasses. Temperatures are forecast to modulate back towards more \nclimatologically-appropriate values in NE KY by Thursday while \nhigher temperatures remain in the forecast for the Cumberland River \nbasin. The resultant temperature gradient is visible in the grids on \nThursday, Friday, and Saturday, which tracks given that forecast \nguidance has trended towards the aforementioned boundary stalling \nout in the vicinity of the area. On the south side of this boundary, \nthere will be additional rounds of showers and storms. The intensity \nand organization of that late-period activity is highly uncertain, \nbut this trend towards a prolonged period of wet weather is \nconcerning. Stay tuned for further forecast updates, and ensure that \nyou have multiple reliable ways to receive watches and warnings \nshould they be needed.",
